555692 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186555157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555692 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6cfeab61-6e76-5a26-9da7-d3d287d0e865" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555693 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555693 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555105 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555693 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555693 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555693 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555573 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555693 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555693 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555693 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186555221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555693 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6f1768e5-8700-506e-98db-0bb9abb61815" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555734 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555734 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555734 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555734 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186555422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555734 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c8685c5a-c976-5c4d-ba32-65fdf5654aca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555761 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555761 #edb/ref 1069 "q-288569" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555761 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555761 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(4r-6)x=22x\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $r?$\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555761 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "06a0435e-893d-5476-8004-8e6faf0aa039" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555761 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=4r-6,\\,b=22.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n4r-6=22.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4r & =28 \\\\ r & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555768 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555768 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86553" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555768 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555768 #edb/ref 1071 "Mike purchased an $80$'s baseball card for $$50.$ If the value of the card increases by $8%$ each year, what expression gives the card's value after $10$ years?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555768 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1b78dc69-6363-5c74-acb7-15e9e0faab04" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555768 #edb/ref 1074 "The card's value is $$50$, and this number increases by $8%$ each year.\n\n$8%$ as a decimal is $0.08.$ Therefore, an increase of $8%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.08=1.08.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- After $1$ year, the value of the card is $50\\cdot (1.08)$ dollars.\n\n- After $2$ years, the value of the card is $50\\cdot (1.08)^{2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $3$ years, the value of the card is $50\\cdot (1.08)^{3}$ dollars.\n\n$\\,⋮$\n\n- After $10$ years, the value of the card is $50\\cdot (1.08)^{10}$ dollars." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555775 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555775 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67769" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555775 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555775 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/16/16e8f82922ebd5d4b10729e2935c893dd59a7dfb8faa69dde4567c7036209d7d.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ in the figure above given that $AC=BC$ and $\\overset{⟷}{CD}⊥\\overset{―}{AB}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555775 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "29fce570-bbfd-5809-b558-27c71c49ba05" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555775 #edb/ref 1074 "By the converse of the perpendicular bisector theorem, a point equidistant from the endpoints of a segment lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment. \n\nHere, the point $C$ is equidistant from the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ Therefore, $\\overset{⟷}{CD}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ \n\nIn particular, we have that $DA=DB.$ We can use this equation so solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}DA & =DB \\\\ 3x-3 & =x+1 \\\\ 3x-x & =1+3 \\\\ 2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555804 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555804 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110680" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555804 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555804 #edb/ref 1071 "Find, in ascending powers of $z$ and descending powers of $x,$ the first three terms of the binomial expansion of ${(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}-z{y}^{2})}^{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555804 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5386af7a-f43c-5f81-bd4e-2f8f31eb31ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555804 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=3,$ $a=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y},$ and $b=-z{y}^{2}.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{3}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,3,3,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}-z{y}^{2})}^{3} & =1{a}^{3}{b}^{0}+3{a}^{2}b+3a{b}^{2}+1{a}^{0}{b}^{3} \\\\  & =1{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})}^{3}{(-z{y}^{2})}^{0}+3{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})}^{2}{(-z{y}^{2})}^{1} \\\\  & \\,+3{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})}^{1}{(-z{y}^{2})}^{2}+1{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})}^{0}{(-z{y}^{2})}^{3} \\\\  & =1(\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}})(1)+3(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}})(-z{y}^{2}) \\\\  & \\,+3(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})({z}^{2}{y}^{4})+1(1)(-{z}^{3}{y}^{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}-\\frac{3{x}^{4}z{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}{y}^{4}}{y}-{z}^{3}{y}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}-3{x}^{4}z+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}{y}^{3}-{z}^{3}{y}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind, in ascending powers of $z$ and descending powers of $x,$ the first three terms are\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}-3{x}^{4}z+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}{y}^{3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555813 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555813 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76803" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555813 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555813 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ff/ff7f82365a0b2253c43ad441a4af93354ae54ae027fbd2390634b64728cfb210.png)\n\nIn the figure shown above, determine the measure of $∠AOB.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555813 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5fcf3b89-deb2-57c7-9289-4881c9262fc3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555813 #edb/ref 1074 "From the diagram, $∠COF$ is a straight angle. So, the angle addition postulate gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠COD+m∠DOE+m∠EOF & =m∠COF \\\\ {75}^{∘}+m∠DOE+{75}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠DOE & ={30}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also note that $∠AOB$ and $∠DOE$ are vertical angles. Therefore, since vertical angles are congruent, we have\n\n$$\nm∠AOB=m∠DOE={30}^{∘}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555821 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555821 #edb/ref 1069 "q-212479" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555821 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555821 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the absolute value equation\n\n$$\n3|x+1|-4=8.\n$$\n\nThis equation can be expressed in the following equivalent form:\n\n$|x+1|=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555821 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "72df5984-46fa-528d-8ad1-e8f21e9e20f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555821 #edb/ref 1074 "We can rearrange the equation to isolate the absolute value on the left side:\n\nFirst, we add $4$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3|x+1|-4 & =8 \\\\ 3|x+1|-4+4 & =8+4 \\\\ 3|x+1| & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3|x+1| & =12 \\\\ \\frac{3|x+1|}{3} & =\\frac{12}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{3}|x+1|}{\\cancel{3}} & =4 \\\\ |x+1| & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555823 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555693 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555823 #edb/ref 1069 "q-22902" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555823 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555823 #edb/ref 1071 "A right triangle has legs of length $2\\,\\text{cm}$ and $4\\,\\text{cm}.$ Calculate the length of its hypotenuse." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555823 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "763919e9-e5c7-5fb3-9ee3-feea81f9fd20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555823 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $a=2\\,cm,$ $b=4\\,cm,$ and $c$ be the length of the hypotenuse. According to the Pythagorean theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}^{2} & ={a}^{2}+{b}^{2} \\\\ c & =\\sqrt{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the values for $a$ and $b$ gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}\\,cm.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555836 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555836 #edb/ref 1069 "q-212910" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555836 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555836 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3\\sqrt{7}}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555836 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "97180f1c-2846-5a93-ae87-17ba0602d531" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555836 #edb/ref 1074 "To remove the radical $\\sqrt{7}$ from the denominator, all we need to do is multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{7}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3\\sqrt{7}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3\\sqrt{7}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{7}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{7}}{3\\cdot (\\sqrt{7})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{7}}{3\\cdot 7} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{7}}{21} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the product rule to simplify the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{7}}{21} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 7}}{21} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{21} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555859 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555859 #edb/ref 1069 "q-54292" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555859 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555859 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ac/ac28d44b12c5804a36cc9ae7a3655237449bfe5f1933df81d70ece5ee18ed981.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nI. $y=-4x+2$\n\nII. $y=x+4$\n\nIII. $y=-2x+4$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555859 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d96a4476-f395-552e-94ef-9cd8de4e9f95" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555859 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,4)$ and $(2,0)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/47/4719dc10bbf4078e6b266a36b50a82e117b3e775395a8a3e44c0dff4ae56f15b.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation I: $y=-4x+2.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4) & =-4(0)+2 \\\\ 4 & =2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-4x+2$ is *not* equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation II: $y=x+4.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$ and $B(2,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4) & =(0)+4 \\\\ 4 & =4\\,✓ \\\\ (0) & =(2)+4 \\\\ 0 & =6\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=x+4$ is *not* equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation III: $y=-2x+4.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$ and $B(2,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4) & =-2(0)+4 \\\\ 4 & =4\\,✓ \\\\ (0) & =-2(2)+4 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-2x+4$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equation that represented the line in the graph was $y=-2x+4,$ corresponding to answer choice III. Therefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555869 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555869 #edb/ref 1069 "q-60049" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555869 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555869 #edb/ref 1071 "Isabella took $2$ hours to wash $12$ windows. If she washes the windows at a steady pace, then what statement describes the unit rate?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555869 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ede2a25e-f545-5338-983c-ea1ddced5b57" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555869 #edb/ref 1074 "The ratio of washed windows to hours is $12:2.$ So we start with the ratio $12:2$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{2}\n$$\n\nWe can make a denominator of $1$ by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12÷2}{2÷2}=\\frac{6}{1}\n$$\n\nConsequently, Isabella washed $6$ windows per hour." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555894 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186555953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555894 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3c2007c4-dec8-59c7-b6b3-8d9aa6d6d7d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555894 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186555761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555898 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186555902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555898 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3ef7c913-7744-593d-a71d-b97eb6eccdee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555898 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186555823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555902 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186555928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555902 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "49b487d5-41bd-53aa-a5f9-930e21fe528b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555902 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186555775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555907 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186555894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555907 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "577ca3a5-901e-518d-a8a8-67bc623b6661" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555907 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186555768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555909 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186555907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555909 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5d4d47af-47b9-5f7a-95fe-8cab364dcf4c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555909 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186555813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555911 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "674d8d23-b9aa-5600-a167-f706632680da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555911 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186555836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555918 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186555909 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555918 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7def1e24-b0f7-53d6-b2ff-05c3d98e74ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555918 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186555804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555920 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "84d2439c-12b2-5f43-8e1a-04cf836fad55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555920 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186555825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555928 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186555954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555928 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b95bbee2-698a-5b3a-8d3b-02cfcf7dc416" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555928 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186555869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555937 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186555940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555937 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cd3ae942-426d-56b9-afd0-f43d65368750" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555937 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555940 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186555920 #edb/ref 175921860